5.Why is constrictor ?


A constructor in Java is a special method that is used to initialize objects when an object of a class is created.
🔹 Key Points about Constructor
1. Constructor name must be the same as the class name
2. It does not have a return type (not even void)
3. It is automatically called when an object is created
4. Used to initialize variables
5. Can be overloaded (multiple constructors in a class)

#  A contractor is special function  present inside the class 


✅ No-Argument Constructor (Default Constructor)

A no-argument constructor is a constructor that does not take any parameters.
🔹 Example:

class Student {
    Student() {
        System.out.println("No-argument constructor called");
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Student s = new Student();
    }
}
✅ Output:

No-argument constructor called

✔️ Use:
* Sets default values
* Runs automatically when object is created
* Easy initialization

✅ Argument Constructor (Parameterized Constructor)

A parameterized constructor is a constructor that takes arguments to initialize values.
🔹 Example:

class Student {
    int id;

    Student(int x) {
        id = x;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Student s = new Student(101);
        System.out.println(s.id);
    }
}
✅ Output:

101

✔️ Use:
* Pass values at runtime
* Initialize object with different data

🔍 Difference Table
No-Argument Constructor	Argument Constructor
No parameters	Takes parameters
Default values	User-defined values
Easy to use	More flexible
Student()	Student(int a)
⭐ Example with Both

class Demo {
    Demo() {
        System.out.println("No argument");
    }

    Demo(int a) {
        System.out.println("Argument: " + a);
    }


    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Demo d1 = new Demo();
        Demo d2 = new Demo(10);
    }
}

